          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-e8f41998f78aeecc\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":4,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Franco directly avows his love and, in the immediate setting of an accepted marriage proposal and ring exchange, also renews and enacts his commitment to Nikki. The romantic and emotional delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance could be read primarily as an affectionate avowal, but the proposal context and explicit description of his voice as “full of promise” strongly support substantial performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Franco directly says “I love you” to Nikki while kissing her immediately after she accepts his marriage proposal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration follows Nikki’s acceptance of Franco’s proposal and occurs as he prepares to place the engagement ring on her finger. It therefore both presents his loving state as true and participates in their newly affirmed marital undertaking. Murmuring it against her lips and repeating it with “so much” mark intimacy and intensity, but the passage does not portray the words as an involuntary or reflex-like discharge.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he murmured against her lips, his voice full of promise.\",\"supports\":\"Direct truth-conditional avowal and strong commitment-bearing force; “full of promise” explicitly connects the utterance to relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Franco asks “Will you marry me?” and Nikki answers, “Yes, I will.”\",\"supports\":\"The love declaration occurs within an explicit proposal-and-acceptance event, strongly supporting performative force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the declaration, Franco slips the ring onto Nikki’s finger, and the narration anticipates “the life they were going to build together.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding action confirms that the declaration accompanies an enacted future-oriented commitment rather than courtship sentiment alone.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you so much, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate repetition reinforces the avowal’s affectionate truth-conditional presentation, without independently indicating reflex-like expressive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the core utterance as both an avowal of love and a commitment-bearing declaration within an engagement ritual. Emotional delivery is adequately treated as context, so neither E nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
